From 1d50a84ae654c87ee497158de778175b83e707ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rehanraza786 <rehanraza786@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:22:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/12] Index-time module hashes, and stop bypassing the path
 index
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two changes that only matter at scale, which is exactly where the
limitations list says nobody has measured yet.

SQLite cannot use an index for `path LIKE 'prefix%'`, because LIKE is
case-insensitive by default; an explicit half-open range can. Verified on a
real index:

  path LIKE 'order-service/%'                       -> SCAN files
  path >= 'order-service/' AND path < 'order-serviceO' -> SEARCH ... USING INDEX

Every prefix query over files is converted (server, enrich, annotate, both
editions) via a shared prefixRange/_prefix_range helper. Infix searches
(`%name%`) are left alone; they cannot use an index either way. Matching
paths case-sensitively is also more correct than LIKE's accidental
case-insensitivity.

Module content hashes are now computed once per index into module_hashes
and read by the server, enrich, and annotate, instead of each recomputing
them per call. That removes a full files scan from every explain() of a
module, and it removes the reason the three implementations could drift
apart — which they already had once, silently, before the sort was
unified. Readers fall back to computing when the table is absent, so an
index built by an older toolkit still answers.
